!set lang_exists=yes


!! present.phtml

!set name_present1=!nosubst You see two pictures below. At the left, the graphs of\
$fcnt functions $fs&nbsp;; at the right, that of a combined function

!set name_present2=!nosubst Your aim is to find out the coefficients of this combination\
(addition), as close as possible. And to do so, you can make up to $maxreply graphic tries.

!! main.phtml

!set name_giveupnote=abandonning your score

!! intro.phtml

!set name_intro1=!nosubst The principle is very simple: if you are given two functions\
\(f_1(x)\) and \(f_2(x)\) as well as two coefficients\
\(c_1\) and \(c_2\), you certainly know how to compute the combination

!set name_intro2=And you know as well that to every function corresponds its graph.\
But on the graphical side, the addition of functions may appear harder,\
as you no longer have obvious means to compute.

!set name_intro3=This is precisely what this exercise proposes: you will be given graphs of\
functions, and asked to find back the coefficients of the combinations leading to these graphs.\
Like many other exercises on WIMS, training by this exercise will allow you to increase\
you ability to graphically recognize functions.

!distribute items Type of functions,Number of tries allowed,Score severity\
into name_introtype, name_introtry,name_severity

!set name_related=Coincidence, functions, addition

